# ======================================================================
# examples.agg  --  the curated DecL example library for v1.0
# ======================================================================
#
# Version: 3
#   (Bump on every meaningful change to the example set. Just count up.)
#
# PURPOSE
# -------
# The small, hand-picked set of examples that is the public face of
# `aggregate`. It is used in three places:
#
#   1. The DEFAULT `build` Underwriter -- what users get out of the box
#      ([build] databases = ["examples"] in config; this is now the
#      shipped default).
#   2. The Cookbook walks through many of these examples
#   3. The Examples dropdown of the aggregate_api (aLL) SPA playground
#      (parsed by aggregate_api/examples.py via GET /v1/examples).
#
# It is deliberately NOT comprehensive -- it shows the IMPORTANT ideas,
# not every DecL feature. Full feature coverage lives in `cookbook.agg`.
#
# FORMAT (read by aggregate_api/examples.py)
# ------------------------------------------
#   * Category titles: one "# <Letter>. <Title>" line per section, A-Z.
#   * Item lines: "<keyword> <Letter>.<Name> ...". The letter files the
#     example under its category; the dot is part of the name.
#   * A "note{...}" becomes the dropdown description; the api picks it out
#     wherever it sits in the statement. Place it on the clause it should
#     attach to when built -- for a `port`, on the `port` header line (a
#     trailing note attaches to the last `agg`, not the portfolio).
#     note{} is NOT legal on `dist` lines -- use a comment.
#   * Statements are separated by a blank line or a trailing ";". A "#" /
#     "//" comment is transparent (it never separates), so it can annotate or
#     comment out a clause inside a multi-line statement.
#   * A statement may span multiple lines with any indentation (used by the
#     portfolios for readability); its lines fold into one logical statement.
#
# Contents
# --------
# A. Showcase -- real-world books (landing-page heroes)
# B. Getting started
# C. Severity objects
# D. Frequency -- Poisson and beyond
# E. Exposure and limit profiles
# F. Reinsurance, gross to net
# G. Portfolios, capital, and a first price
# H. Profit and loss -- signed support
# I. Dependent risks
# J. Numerical character -- tails and windowing
# K. Distortion (risk measure)


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# A. Showcase
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The landing-page heroes -- examples that show the range in
# one line each: a cat ILW tower, a P&C portfolio, an exposure-rated
# limit profile, and a net/ceded XOL view. (Drafts -- amounts and grids
# still being tuned; some carry heavy-severity validation notes.)
agg A.CatXOLTower
    1.74 claims
    sev lognorm 8.501 cv 14.624
    occurrence net of
        90% po 200 xs 200 and
        95% po 400 xs 400
    poisson
    hints{bs=2;log2=16;normalize=False;}
    note{ILW model on US Cat, amounts in billions.}

port A.PropCas note{Portfolio of property and casualty risks, amounts in 000s.}
    agg Casualty
        5000 premium at 90% lr
        5000 xs 0
        sev lognorm 200 cv 8
        mixed gamma .45
    agg Property
        4000 premium at 85% lr
        10000 xs 0
        sev lognorm 250 cv 12
        mixed gamma .15

agg A.ExposureRating
    [100 200 1000 50] premium  at
    [.9 .85 .9 .8] lr
    [250 500 1000 2000] xs 0
    sev lognorm 120 cv 12
    occurrence ceded to
        750 xs 750
    mixed gamma .2
    note{Exposure rating on a small portfolio with limits profile, amounts in 000s.}

netceded
    agg A.NC
        8 claims
        sev 300 * beta 2 3
        occurrence net of
            0.7 so 60 xs 40
        poisson
    note{Bivariate distribution of net and ceded for XOL reinsurance program.}

agg A.Discrete
    dfreq[1 2 3 4 5 6 7 20]
    dsev [-10 -5 -2 -1 0 1 2 3] [1/16 1/16 1/8 1/8 1/8 1/4 1/8 1/8]
    note{Discrete example with signed severity; empirical distributions can be used directly!}

agg Tweedie
  25 claims
  sev gamma 1 cv .05
  poisson
  note{Tweedie - Poisson sum of gammas. Extreme case, severity has low CV.}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# B. Getting started
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Discrete inputs make the method's promise concrete: the answer is
# EXACT and the validation error columns are identically zero.
agg B.ThreeDice
    dfreq [3]
    dsev [1:6]
    note{Sum of three dice -- exact discrete aggregate; zero validation error.};

# Two more discrete idioms: explicit [outcomes] [probabilities] for the
# frequency, and a stepped range [start:stop:step] for the severity.
agg B.Discrete
    dfreq [1 2 3] [.5 .3 .2]
    dsev [2:10:2]
    note{Explicit [outcomes] [probs] frequency; severity on the stepped grid 2, 4, 6, 8, 10.};

# The canonical continuous compound: Poisson frequency, lognormal
# severity, capped per occurrence -- the book used throughout the intro.
agg B.Basic
    250 claims
    1000 xs 0
    sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5
    poisson
    note{Plain Poisson / lognormal aggregate -- the workhorse compound distribution.}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# C. Severity objects
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two standalone, named severity curves. (Other severity features --
# mixtures, empirical data, limits, splicing -- are shown inside the
# Aggregate examples in the sections below, where they actually bite.)
#
# Pareto: the classic cat / large-loss curve, parameterized scale * shape
# then shifted to ground-up support. Heavy and a little fiddly.
sev C.Pareto
    1000 * pareto 1.8 - 1000
    note{Single-parameter Pareto large-loss curve (scale 1000, shape 1.8), shifted to ground-up.};

# Mixed exponential: the curve US actuaries know from ISO/Verisk size-of-
# loss tables. NOTE this one CANNOT be materialized as a standalone Sev
# object (a mixture has no single frozen scipy rv) -- aggregate stores
# the spec and you fold it into an `agg`, e.g. the E.Mixed pattern. A bit
# grungy, but it is what it is.
sev C.MixedExp
    [10 50 200] * expon wts [.6 .3 .1]
    note{Mixed exponential (ISO/Verisk style). Can't stand alone as a Sev -- fold it into an agg.}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# D. Frequency -- Poisson and beyond
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same severity, two frequency assumptions, side by side. Real claim
# counts are over-dispersed: years differ for shared reasons (weather,
# inflation, the legal climate). `mixed gamma 0.4` puts a gamma
# multiplier (CV 0.4) on the Poisson mean -- a negative binomial by
# another road -- and it drives required capital up materially.
agg D.Poisson
    100 claims
    1000 xs 0
    sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5
    poisson
    note{Poisson frequency -- the no-contagion baseline.};

agg D.MixedGamma
    100 claims
    1000 xs 0
    sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5
    mixed gamma 0.4
    note{Same severity, over-dispersed counts (gamma-mixed Poisson = negative binomial).};

# A bounded count: binomial n = 20, p = 0.5 (mean 10).
agg D.Binomial
    10 claims
    dsev [1]
    binomial 0.5
    note{Bounded claim count -- binomial (here n = 20, p = 0.5).}

# Zero-modified Poisson -- inflate the no-claim probability. COMMENTED
# OUT pending the ZT/ZM redesign (TODO B4: input the base mean, ship a
# shift helper); counts toward the set but does not run in tests.
# agg D.ZMPoisson 4 claims dsev [1] poisson zm 0.5 note{Zero-modified Poisson -- inflate the probability of a no-claim year.}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# E. Exposure and limit profiles
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Volume can be a claim count, an expected loss, or a premium-at-a-loss-
# ratio -- and these vectorize into a profile.
agg E.ByLoss
    500 loss
    sev lognorm 50 cv 0.8
    poisson
    note{State expected loss; the claim count is derived.};

# THE BIG ONE: a premium x limit x loss-ratio table (the shape every
# pricing actuary already has). aggregate derives the implied claim
# count per row so the limit bands are weighted correctly.
agg E.LimitProfile
    [1000 2000 500] prem at [.8 .7 .5] lr
    [1000 2000 5000] xs 0
    sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5
    poisson
    note{Premium x limit x loss-ratio profile -- implied claim counts computed per band.};

# Mixed exposure + mixed severity + mixed frequency in one book: the
# shared frequency mixer correlates the components (visible in stats_df).
agg E.Mixed
    [1000 2000 500] prem at [.8 .7 .5] lr
    sev lognorm 10 cv [.35 .5] wts [5/8 3/8]
    mixed gamma 0.5
    note{Mixed exposure, severity, and frequency -- shared mixing shows up in stats_df.}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# F. Reinsurance, gross to net
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Occurrence (per-claim) and aggregate (per-year) programs are each one
# clause. Shares read naturally: "50% po 2500 xs 2500" is half of the
# 2500-xs-2500 layer (po = part of).
agg F.OccXOL
    15 claims
    10000 xs 0
    sev lognorm 1500 cv 1.0
    occurrence net of
        50% po 2500 xs 2500 and
        90% po 5000 xs 5000
    mixed gamma 0.25
    note{Occurrence excess-of-loss program, net view.};

agg F.Ceded
    1250 premium at 80% lr
    1000 xs 0 sev
    lognorm 80 cv 1.675
    occurrence ceded to
        125 xs 0 and
        50% po 125 xs 125 and
        20% po 250 xs 250 and
        5% po 500 xs 500
    poisson
    note{Layered occurrence reinsurance tower, ceded view.};

agg F.AggStop
    100 claims
    1000 xs 0
    sev lognorm 100 cv 1.5
    poisson
    aggregate net of
        5000 xs 30000
    note{Aggregate stop-loss -- net of an annual 5000-xs-30000 layer.};

# The joint gross / ceded / net law of a reinsured book in one object.
netceded
    agg F.NetCeded
        15 claims
        10000 xs 0
        sev lognorm 1500 cv 1.0
        occurrence net of
            50% po 2500 xs 2500 and
            90% po 5000 xs 5000
        mixed gamma 0.25
        note{Joint gross / ceded / net view of the reinsured book.}

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# G. Portfolios, capital, and a first price
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# A `port` convolves independent units onto one grid; diversification
# becomes arithmetic and capital-loaded pricing is one call
# (port.price_pentagon). The tail-mix of the units is the whole story,
# so we sweep thin/thin, thick/thin, and thick/thick deliberately.
# Written multi-line for readability: a statement spans as many lines as you
# like; a blank line or a trailing ; separates one statement from the next.
port G.Book
    agg Property 80 claims  500 xs 0 sev lognorm  50 cv 1.2 poisson
    agg Casualty 20 claims 2000 xs 0 sev lognorm 250 cv 2.0 mixed gamma 0.4
    note{Two-unit book: thin property + mixed-frequency casualty. Capital and price downstream.};

# thick + thin: a heavy but BOUNDED cat line (limited, so finite moments,
# yet genuinely fat in the body) diversifying a stable attritional line.
# This is where the interesting allocation behaviour lives.
port G.ThickThin
    agg NonCat 200 claims 100 xs 0 sev gamma   8 cv 0.30 poisson
    agg Cat      2 claims 5000 xs 0 sev lognorm 500 cv 4.0 poisson
    note{Thin attritional + bounded-but-thick cat -- the rich diversification case.};

# thin + thin: two tightly concentrated units (the PIR Tame shape);
# the total is nearly Gaussian.
port G.ThinThin
    agg A 1 claim sev gamma 50 cv 0.10 fixed
    agg B 1 claim sev gamma 50 cv 0.15 fixed
    note{Two thin, concentrated units -- nearly Gaussian total.};

# thick + thick: two unbounded heavy lognormals; diversification is weak
# and the tail dominates.
port G.ThickThick
    agg A 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 3.0 poisson
    agg B 100 claims sev lognorm 80 cv 3.5 poisson
    note{Two unbounded heavy-tailed units -- little diversification, tail-driven.};

# Common shock: two units sharing the SAME gamma frequency mixer are
# positively correlated (the dependence cat modelers expect).
port G.Shared
    agg A 100 claims sev lognorm 50 cv 1.5 mixed gamma 0.4
    agg B 120 claims sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 mixed gamma 0.4
    note{Shared frequency mixer = common shock; correlated units on one grid.};

# A discrete allocation thought-experiment (Bodoff): bounded atoms make
# capital allocation hand-checkable.
port G.Bodoff
    agg Wind  1 claim dsev [0 99]  [0.80 0.20] fixed
    agg Quake 1 claim dsev [0 100] [0.95 0.05] fixed
    note{Bodoff thought-experiment -- discrete two-peril allocation.}

# PLACEHOLDERS for the two sample-driven portfolios (build-from-sample
# and the Sample "switcheroo"). These are a Python-API construction, not
# pure DecL, so they are reserved here pending the sampling redesign
# (TODO T3). Slots held; not yet runnable.
# port G.FromSample  <build a Portfolio from an external loss sample>
# port G.Switcheroo  <swap a unit's marginal for a sampled one>


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# H. Profit and loss -- signed support
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# A profit is a negative loss. The `pnl` keyword builds premium-minus-
# loss; a dsev with a negative atom carries genuine signed support and
# auto-windows around a possibly-negative mean.

agg H.Signed dfreq [3] dsev [-2 5] [.5 .5] note{Signed severity -- negative outcomes allowed (closed-form support).}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# I. Dependent risks
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Couple two perils' per-claim severities with a copula and accumulate
# them under a shared frequency (a 2D FFT). Marginalizing one axis
# recovers that peril's standalone aggregate.
bivariate I.Copula 25 claims agg Wind dfreq [0 1] [.3 .7] sev lognorm 40 cv 1.2 agg Flood dfreq [0 1] [.5 .5] sev lognorm 60 cv 1.5 copula gumbel 0.4 poisson note{Gumbel-copula dependence between Wind and Flood.}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# J. Numerical character -- tails and windowing
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Power-law (Pareto) tails are sub-exponential and unforgiving. With
# infinite variance the auto-sizer balks and asks you to choose the grid
# (hints{}); even then some tail mass cannot be represented, and the
# validator SAYS SO rather than handing back a silently wrong number.
agg J.Heavy 10 claims sev 100 * pareto 1.3 - 100 poisson hints{bs=0.25; log2=16} note{Infinite-variance Pareto -- the validator refuses to guess.};
# Frequency-driven concentration: a very high-mean integer book whose
# mass band clears zero is resolved on a two-sided window FAR from the
# origin, so bs sizes off the band width, not x_max.
agg J.Windowed 10000000 claims dsev [1 2] poisson note{Concentrated high-mean book -- resolved on a non-zero output window.};
# Premium minus loss at high frequency: a payoff severity (constant minus
# a loss) summed over many claims tends to Gaussian and lives on a signed
# window -- the central-limit end of the spectrum.
agg J.PmL 5000 claims ssev 100 - lognorm 80 cv .2 poisson note{High-frequency premium-minus-loss payoff -- near-Gaussian on a signed window.};
# Tweedie: a Poisson sum of gammas, stated by mean, power p, dispersion
# -- the pure-premium workhorse.
agg J.Tweedie tweedie 1 1.005 0.1 note{Tweedie by mean, power p, and dispersion (variance = dispersion * mean**p).}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# K. Distortion (risk measure)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# A distortion is a spectral risk measure that loads loss into price.
# (note{} is not legal on dist lines -- describe them in comments.)
# Proportional-hazard distortion (universally understood).
dist K.PH ph 0.7;
# A dual-power distortion, the second leaf for the minimum below.
dist K.Dual dual 2.5;
# A minimum (the more conservative envelope) of the two named leaves.
# Combo distortions reference children by name, so this must come AFTER
# K.PH and K.Dual and is resolved by sequential load (not a fresh KB).
dist K.Min minimum dist.K.PH dist.K.Dual
